<h2>The legal foundation</h2>
<p>Section 11 of the MSMED Act empowers the Central or State Government to notify preference policies in respect of procurement of goods and services produced and provided by micro and small enterprises. The 2012 Order is the Central notification under that power; a 2018 amendment raised the mandatory share to 25 per cent and added the sub-target for women-owned MSEs. Because the Order derives from the statute, tender conditions inconsistent with it stand on weak ground — a point that distinguishes it from purely executive procurement guidelines.</p>

<h2>The entitlements, organised</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Entitlement</th><th>Content</th><th>Who can claim</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Procurement share</td><td>25% of annual procurement from MSEs; 4% SC/ST-owned; 3% women-owned</td><td>MSEs with valid Udyam registration</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tender documents</td><td>Free of cost</td><td>Registered MSEs</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Earnest money</td><td>Exemption from EMD/bid security</td><td>Registered MSEs</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Purchase preference</td><td>MSEs within L1 + 15% band may supply a portion at L1 price in divisible tenders</td><td>MSEs meeting technical specifications</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reserved items</td><td>Items notified for exclusive procurement from MSEs</td><td>MSEs manufacturing the notified items</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vendor development</td><td>Buyer-seller meets, vendor development programmes by CPSEs</td><td>MSE sector generally</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Where disputes actually arise</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Eligibility barriers</h3><p>Tenders prescribing turnover or prior-experience thresholds that exclude MSEs wholesale. The policy directs relaxation consistent with quality requirements; a blanket threshold untested against that direction is the classic challenge.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>EMD demanded despite exemption</h3><p>Procuring entities sometimes demand bid security from registered MSEs or reject bids for non-payment. A pre-bid clarification citing the Order usually cures it; rejection on this ground alone is vulnerable.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Purchase preference denied</h3><p>MSEs quoting within the 15 per cent band refused the L1-matching opportunity in divisible procurements, or tenders declared indivisible without reasons. The record of reasons is where such challenges are won.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Classification contests</h3><p>Rivals questioning a bidder&rsquo;s MSE status, or entities refusing to accept Udyam certificates. Classification follows the investment and turnover criteria of the Act, and the certificate is the operative evidence.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Enforcement, calibrated</h2>
<p>The remedial ladder matters because tender timelines are unforgiving. A representation at the pre-bid stage — specific, clause-referenced, seeking amendment of the offending condition — creates the record on which everything later stands. If the condition survives, the choice is between bidding under protest and moving the High Court before the bid deadline; judicial review of tender conditions is deferential, but breach of a statutory Order is precisely the ground on which it bites. After award, the challenge becomes materially harder: courts weigh the successful bidder&rsquo;s equities and the public interest in completed procurement, and relief tends toward directions for future compliance rather than unwinding contracts. The practical rule for MSE bidders is therefore early vigilance — read the tender against the Order on day one, not after disqualification.</p>

<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Keep Udyam registration current and ensure the classification reflects the latest investment and turnover position.</li>
  <li>Quote the Udyam number in every bid and invoke the specific exemptions in the bid covering letter.</li>
  <li>Document pre-bid queries and answers; they are the foundation of any later challenge.</li>
  <li>Remember the payment side: once supply begins, Sections 15-18 of the MSMED Act and the Samadhaan mechanism govern delayed payments even against government buyers.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Procurement grievances are won on speed and specificity. An MSE that identifies the offending clause, cites the 2012 Order provision it violates, and moves — administratively and, if needed, judicially — before bids close, preserves every remedy. One that waits for the award letter has usually kept only the lesson.</p>
</div>
